ABSTRACT = ("Early sepsis warning models need many costly labelled intensive care unit stays. "
"Self-supervised pretraining can use unlabelled records, but generic objectives ignore known physiology. "
"We propose physiology-guided temporal-consistency self-supervised learning, which combines organ-system block masking, "
"a consistency loss built only from physiological relations validated in the data, and temporal representation consistency. "
"We evaluated it on 40,336 patients from two hospital systems in the PhysioNet/Computing in Cardiology Challenge 2019 data, "
"after finding that three documented units do not match the recorded values. "
"Across ten random seeds and label budgets from 1% to 100%, with a hierarchical bootstrap and Holm correction, the method was not "
"significantly better in area under the receiver operating characteristic curve than generic masked reconstruction, "
"a supervised recurrent network or gradient-boosted trees. A full factorial ablation showed that temporal consistency was the "
"only component with a measurable effect (+0.018 with 5% of labels). A supervised recurrent network that saw only which variables "
"were measured, and when, outperformed every network given the full input. Pretraining helped in two settings: transfer from the "
"hospital that measures blood gases often to the one that rarely does (+0.050 over generic pretraining with 10% of labels), "
"and pretraining of a Transformer encoder (+0.040). Gradient-boosted trees had the best clinical utility at every budget, and "
"recalibration on validation data closed the calibration gap of the networks. The physiological prior added little beyond the "
"measurement pattern; such priors need testing against strong tabular baselines, many seeds and several hospitals.")

INDEX_TERMS = "Clinical time series, early warning systems, intensive care units, label efficiency, machine learning, physiological constraints, self-supervised learning, sepsis, transfer learning"

